# ============================================================================
# tools/hls-reverify.hls — Stage 108 memory-safety re-verification under
# `-O fast` (proof replay), in Halis — the native twin of the Python
# hls-reverify.py + hlreverify_parts/ that retire.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Usage:
#   bin/hls-reverify FILE [--lto] [--json] [--out DIR]
#   bin/hls-reverify selftest
#   bin/hls-reverify selftest identities|forget|forge|dangle
#       (the battery's old python-driver reaches, restated natively:
#       the pinned identity whitelist, the for-get discriminator, the
#       forged-annotation audit, the dangling-operand integrity vector)
#
# Under `-O fast`, the compiler ELIDES runtime memory-safety checks —
# bounds, division-by-zero, int64 overflow — wherever the interval
# prover PROVED them dead. The proofs are computed once, on the checked
# AST. Everything downstream — the HLIR optimiser (constant folding,
# copy propagation, DCE, inlining, LICM), every backend — is trusted to
# preserve them. That trust is exactly the kind of hole a verifier
# exists to close. This tool closes it with a REPLAY:
#
#  1. BASELINE — the checked program's memory-safety evidence: every
#     check site (ovf / div / bnd) with the interval prover's verdict
#     (the ovf_safe / div_safe / bnd_safe annotations the checker's own
#     proof pass wrote — src/hlc/proof.hls, the ONE definition). Proven
#     sites are the claims `-O fast` may elide; checked sites keep
#     their runtime check.
#
#  2. TRANSFORM — the program is lowered to HLIR and run through the
#     optimiser (fast=True; --lto raises the cross-crate inline
#     threshold), exactly as a fast build would.
#
#  3. REPLAY — an independent abstract interpreter re-derives a verdict
#     for every check site the TRANSFORMED IR still contains: the same
#     seeds (the requires clauses, through seed_from_requires), the
#     same interval arithmetic (iv_add / iv_sub / iv_mul / iv_join /
#     widen_growth_iv — the proof module imported via hlfront, never
#     re-implemented), joins at merges, widening at loop headers, a
#     post-fixpoint containment verification. Plus the integrity of the
#     transformed IR itself: every operand resolves, every label
#     resolves, every def precedes its use.
#
#  4. THE LAW (fail-closed) — a proven claim discharges when its site
#     re-proves or when the optimiser eliminated the site (folded to a
#     constant / dead code — no runtime operation remains). A proven
#     claim whose site SURVIVES but no longer re-proves is a LOST-PROOF:
#     the verdict fails, exit 1. A `safe_overflow` annotation neither an
#     interval proof nor a pinned algebraic identity certifies is a
#     FORGED ANNOTATION: exit 1. A dangling operand, a dangling label,
#     an unreachable block: BROKEN-IR, exit 1.
#
# What changed with the native era: the .py drove the retired
# interpreter's dict-AST (boot.load_program / boot.check) and the
# retired tools/ir layer; this port rides the REAL front-end
# (tools/hlfront.hls — hl_load_crate + hl_check, the same fixpoint
# bin/hlc runs) and re-derives the HLIR builder + optimiser over the
# native ExprN/StmtN pools (the retired tools/ir/__init__.py +
# optimize.py, ported as the structured RVInstr/RVBlock layer below —
# the same block shapes, temp naming and pass order hlemit's --emit ir
# printer carries). The interval law stays ONE DEFINITION: the replay
# imports the native proof module's arithmetic and seeding for BOTH the
# baseline verdicts and the replay engine.
#
# One honest restatement: the retired engine encoded the symbolic
# `i < xs.len()` bound as a tuple interval bound that DIED at the first
# merge; the native prover (the one definition now) carries such bounds
# in a lenhi map that survives joins when both sides agree. The replay
# rides the native discipline — the lenhi map, joined by the prover's
# own join laws — so a claim the compiler's proof pass makes is a claim
# the replay can still see on the other side of the optimiser.
#
# Two more, both the .py-era engine's blind spots the native prover
# outgrew:
#   - the native iv_fits treats a bound EQUAL to the INT64_MAX/INT64_MIN
#     sentinel as unknown (deep-scan-10's law; python used None
#     sentinels, so [0, max-1] + [1, 1] "fitted"). The selftest's
#     corner vector pins the native refusal.
#   - the native proof pass (Stage 98) runs for ANY function carrying
#     refinements — body-level `let x: int where ...` guards included —
#     so the baseline holds claims the .py-era pass (requires/param
#     preds only) never made. The IR port stamps the guard's pred text
#     on the refined load/store (the same text->expr map the checker's
#     param_preds use) and the replay re-derives the post-guard facts
#     through the ONE definition (refine_pred_interval /
#     seed_refine_nz) — a refined binding is a fact boundary the copy
#     propagator never rewrites past.
#
# Exit contract: 0 REPLAY-OK; 1 REPLAY-FAILED or a compile refusal; 2
# usage errors. Read-only end to end (writes only when --out names a
# report directory). --json prints the machine report (schema
# hls-reverify-report/v1, canonical JSON — sorted keys, no whitespace,
# byte-identical over an unchanged tree). A program that fails to parse
# or check is a clean refusal ("compile error: ..."), never a crash:
# the front-end runs in a probe child that re-executes this binary
# (--probe-check), the hls-audit pattern.
# ============================================================================

import "./hlfront.hls"
import "std.str"

# ============================================================================
# Constants — hlrev_common.py's own.
# ============================================================================

fn rv_version() -> str {
    return "0.127.0-alpha"
}

fn rv_report_schema() -> str {
    return "hls-reverify-report/v1"
}

# Check-site kinds — the exact elision surface of `-O fast`.
